<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#dharmachakra">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharmachakra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dharmachakra, or Wheel of the Law, is a Buddhist symbol representing the teaching of the Buddha, recalling his first sermon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, known as setting the wheel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> in motion. It is usually shown as a spoked wheel, often with eight spokes for the Eightfold Path. In the Dvaravati culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> large stone wheels were set on pillars at temple sites.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dharmachakra.jpg" alt="Dharmachakra" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: bn (User talk:Armanaziz) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka_Chakra_Y.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
